Hollywood has always managed to cash in  with genre pictures when it comes to  cinemas biggest bang for the buck  however Studios big and small continue  to mine the spooky blood-spattered realm  of horror few filmmakers have been able  to get more from a meager budget than  Eduardo Sanchez and Daniel Merrik when  the pair set out to turn their 35 page  outline into a feature film in 1997 they  cobbled together a mere $25,000 that  modest budget gave the first-time  filmmakers just enough loot to hire  three completely unknown actors dropped  them off in the woods with a couple of  borrowed cameras and hoped that their  efforts and an out-of-the-box concept  would be enough to turn that rough  outline into a genuinely upsetting  found-footage chiller it was more than  enough the buzz behind the Blair Witch  Project was so real that audiences  flocked to theaters the summer of 1999  to see it many still believed it to be  legit found footage which had a lot to  do with a radical marketing campaign  that never quite confirmed or denied the  movie's authenticity that the film  itself is such a viscerally unsettling  experience in its own right  only fanned the flames and the  combination turned blair witch into an  unexpected box-office behemoth with  nearly two hundred and fifty million  dollars in worldwide ticket sales it  ranks among the most profitable movies  ever produced John Carpenter's Halloween  stands as one of the most revered horror  movies ever made the near-flawless 1978  film essentially gave birth to the  slasher genre but it also paved the way  for meticulously crafted character  driven scares and as it happens  carpenters groundbreaking slasher more  than earns its spot on this list because  the horror master himself had a mere  three hundred and twenty five thousand  dollars to bring his frightful vision to  life NAT slim budget is of course part  of what helped Halloween stand out it  forced carpenter to use innovative  techniques like that Steadicam  prospective opening or taking a simple  jumpsuit and a William Shatner mask and  turning it into a seemingly inhuman  monster though carpenter and as many  ghastly creations have continued to  spook us over the years few have  unsettled us quite like this  micro-budget masterpiece has and even  fewer have genuinely stood the test of  time with such monstrous eloquence these  days we all know jason blum blum house  productions as the powerhouse behind  genre hits like sinister splits get out  and the purge prior to 2007 however Blum  house was known mostly as a company that  wanted to produce high  genre movies with little to no budget  with a few non-professional actors a  home video camera a 7-day shooting  schedule and a fifteen thousand dollar  budgets Paranormal Activity didn't just  fit the parameters for what Blum House  was trying to do it essentially became  the blueprints Oren Paley's haunting  tale of a young couple tormented by a  malevolent demonic presence was the film  that officially put Blum housed on the  Hollywood map once the film ran through  Paramount Pictures and into wide  distribution Paranormal Activity scared  up an astonishing 193 million dollars at  the box office I think anyone sitting  disabilities being successful for the  record those numbers also earned the  film the title of the most profitable  movie ever made  with eyebrow racing numbers like that it  wasn't long before people started  throwing the word sequel around instead  Paley and Blum houses start no budget  vision birthed an entire franchise to  date the Paranormal Activity verse has  spawned a total of five sequels that  have brought in close to a billion  dollars in worldwide ticket sales not a  bad return on an initial $15,000  investment though he's dabbled almost  exclusively in the realm of tentpole  Pictures over the last couple of decades  Sam Raimi spent their early days of his  career as a low-budget Jon ray Wiz with  a dizzying pension for delivering both  big laughs and bloody carnage with equal  fervor 1981 s ghoul fest the Evil Dead  delivered a genuinely disturbing  altogether unforgettable cinematic  experience Raimi put together the  effects-heavy film with the reported  budget of only three hundred and fifty  thousand dollars while it didn't exactly  set the domestic box-office ablaze the  Evil Dead more than turned to tidy  profit upon release and helps set a new  standard for the growing wave of shock  and schlock horrid devotees more than  anything approved Raimi to be a  filmmaker of singular cinematic vision  and it remains ground zero for the wild  stylistic flourishes unapologetic camp  and relentless bloodletting that would  inform virtually every one of his films  thereafter oh and did we mention that  the Evil Dead also made a bona fide cult  sensation of its chisel chin star Bruce  Campbell  make no mistake when George a Romero's  zombie classic Night of the Living Dead  hit theaters back in 1968 it was like  nothing that had ever come before it's  the film was so singular and vision and  execution that it basically invented a  new horror sub-genre and moviegoers have  been devouring tales of the flesh-eating  undead ever since coming to get you  Barbara  of course Night of the Living Dead  wasn't always meant to be game changing  horror Romero's feature debut was  originally supposed to be a sci-fi  comedy about hot rodding aliens when  budget constraints made that concept all  but impossible the idea pivoted to  flesh-eating aliens it finally ended up  on undead flesh-eating humans which  allowed the production to make full use  of its paltry 114 thousand dollar budget  to say Romero got his money's worth  would be an understatement  the film's gritty style grisly special  effects inviting social commentary hit  home with genre fans and casual film  goers alike and brought in a staggering  18 million dollars at the box office  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite stuff or coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

